{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6d7","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06f86-1896-77b0-a64d-49a48c76fe78"},"sequence":454,"recorded_at":1780531400000903,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"3bc73467-9e0e-4b04-8606-b581be737d17","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"3bc73467-9e0e-4b04-8606-b581be737d17","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"3bc73467-9e0e-4b04-8606-b581be737d17"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"d0c269a5-af86-422e-996c-ae7816a88c39"},"task_id":"d0c269a5-af86-422e-996c-ae7816a88c39","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"d0c269a5-af86-422e-996c-ae7816a88c39","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-16-1\",\n  \"command\": \"ls .agents/bin/ 2>&1; cat LOGBOOK.md 2>&1 | head -n 100; echo ===TRACECHECK===; find . -name \\\"*tracecheck*\\\" -not -path \\\"./.git/*\\\" 2>/dev/null | head; grep -rn \\\"tracecheck\\\" --include=\\\"*.go\\\" --include=\\\"*.md\\\" --include=\\\"*.json\\\" . 2>/dev/null | grep -v \\\".git/\\\" | head -n 20\",\n  \"description\": \"Find tracecheck and logbook\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"scan_scope_hint\": \"[hint] unscoped recursive scan over the workspace root — scope the next search to the subtree the task names or use the bounded search tool; if this scan backgrounded, harvest or terminate it before ending the turn.\",\n  \"output\": \"ls: .agents/bin/: No such file or directory\\n# Flight Logbook\\n\\n> Institutional memory. Concise, factual, high-signal.\\n> Newest entries first. One block per insight.\\n\\n## 2026-09-05\\n\\n### 0435 — Provider JSONL host leaf round-2 rework: arm identity, exact bounds, production-site pins\\n\\n- SCOPE: TASK-260830-qcosxq round-1 verdict (changes requested, 44/66 killed, 18 real survivors). Test-only rework in `internal/provhost` plus README/LOGBOOK honesty items; production untouched. Uncommitted per the CR-shape instruction.\\n- FIX (F25, structural, first): every refusal row now names its arm — `requireFrameRefusal` (code + `member` detail + rule detail + non-retryable) for frame refusals, `requireLocalRefusal` (code + rule detail + non-retryable) for invalid_config/integrity/timeout/process arms, plus `observed`-version assertions on both mismatch paths and `status_state` on unknown. Zero code-only refusal rows remain outside the two helpers, the bound-child surfacing check, and mismatch checks paired with identity assertions. Matches the sibling leaf's 0/29 → all-asserted shape.\\n- FIX (F21): five foreign-major malformed versions (`3.0`, `3.0.0.0`, `.0.0`, `3..0`, `3.b.c`) asserting `provider_protocol_error`; each of the four deleted `parseMajor` arms now promotes its shape to `incompatible_protocol` and reddens.\\n- FIX (F22): token floor pinned two-sided — 31 decoded bytes refused, 32 accepted. Floors `< 8`, `< 16` killed by the 31-byte row; `< 48` killed by the 32-byte row and the 38-byte valid fixture.\\n- FIX (F23): `readCapped` cap path tested both halves — over-cap cut to exactly cap bytes with prefix content (kills truncation removal), error-past-the-cap never reached (kills limiter removal), under/exact-cap passthrough.\\n- FIX (F24): request side gains the exact-boundary test the response side had — limit+1 refused with arm identity, limit accepted. Halving the request bound reddens.\\n- FIX (F26): `exit 3` / `exit 0` silent plugins driven through production `ExecRunner` + `Host.Call`; the both-sites-cleared exit-code mutant reproduces the reviewer's exact `process_failed` → `protocol_error` flip and is killed.\\n- FIX (F27): stdin capture through production `ExecRunner` asserts frame + one `\\\\n`; the omitted-terminator mutant reddens.\\n- FIX (F28/F29): orphaned discovery paragraph moved back above the JSONL section; the \\\"proven by narrowing mutants\\\" sentence restated to what the tests pin (arm identities, 31/32 token bytes, both exact frame bounds).\\n- FINDING: the crash-exit test as first written was flaky — a plugin that exits without draining stdin races stdin writes into EPIPE, surfacing `runner reported no result` instead of the exit-code path. Fixed by draining stdin in the fixture scripts; the flake is in the fixture, not production.\\n- EQUIVALENT (declared, not chased): M09b — narrowing the `checkResponseMembers` required list to drop `\\\"ok\\\"` changes nothing observable, because the missing-`ok` pre-check in `DecodeResponse` fires first on every input missing `ok`. The list entry is unreachable defense in depth. Single-site R17 (clearing only the `ExitError`-branch reassignment) is likewise a no-op because the `Result` literal already carries `ProcessState.ExitCode()`; the behavior-bearing mutant clears both sites and is killed.\\n- EVIDENCE: 19-mutant verify sweep (`/tmp/rev2-verify.sh`, cp-aside/cp-back per mutant, presence asserted, restore byte-verified): 18 killed, 1 declared equivalent (M09b). True two-site R17 verified killed separately with the reviewer's exact flip. `go test ./...` exit 0 (15/15), `-race` on provhost exit 0, `go vet` and `GOOS=windows go vet` exit 0, gofmt gate clean, `go build` on linux/windows exit 0, `tracecheck` exit 0 unchanged (17/403, 74 cases). provhost cover 86.1% → 88.1%.\\n- BOUND (stated plainly, widened the wrong way): `provhost` does not import `internal/provider` (only a comment at `runner.go:44`), so this leaf is not the first production caller; `provhost` itself has no production caller and there is no `cmd/ax`. Two orphaned packages, every `Host.Call` assertion made from a test. The non-retryability sentence in `doc.go` is now asserted on every refusal row rather than standing on prose.\\n- STATUS: Ready for review, handed off uncommitted.\\n\\n### 0429 — Provider JSONL host leaf: protocol transport, three inherited findings, one repeated harness failure\\n\\n- SCOPE: TASK-260830-qcosxq (story leaf 2). New `internal/provhost` (§7.2 framing/lifecycle, §7.5 dispatch names, status recovery; bodies opaque except status rules), plus F18/F19/F20 closures in `internal/provider` and `.github/workflows/ci.yml`. Uncommitted per the CR-shape instruction.\\n- FINDING: F18 closed with the verdict's four-line pin (`ownerAttester` pointer-equals `fileOwnerUID`); SEAM1 verified red before restore. F19 closed by planting a distinct-ID candidate in each absolute dir of `TestDiscoverRefusesRelativePluginDir`; the `return out` mutant returns 1 candidate and reddens. F20 closed with a `GOOS=windows go vet ./...` CI step; a planted type error in `os_windows_test.go` verified the step sees the file.\\n- FINDING: the §7.3/§7.5 operation registry counts 15, not 16 — the manifest JSON and the §7.5 table both list manifest, probe, launch, identify-session, quiesce, native-store-plan, capture, materialize, materialize-status, materialize-commit, materialize-rollback, resume, fork, stop, doctor. The ownership gap prose saying \\\"16-entry\\\" miscounts; the registry test derives the 15 from the pinned table in order.\\n- ANOMALY: repeated entry 2113 exactly — the mutant sweep restored via `git checkout` on untracked files, silently no-opping, so mutants accumulated and every verdict after the first was taken on a red mask. Caught because later mutants stopped compiling. Recovery: byte-level reversal of every applied pair (one 2x over-replace repaired by hand), then a clean re-run with `cp` backups, per-mutant presence assertions, and restore verification.\\n- DECISION: transport lives in `internal/provhost`, not `internal/provider` — leaf 1's `TestDiscoveryReachesNoProcess` fails any `os/exec` import in the discovery package, and weakening that scan would resurrect a round-3 kill. Local failures are `*axerror.Error` via six instrumented constructors (audited bidirectionally with a closed six-code set); child failures surface through `DecodeBound` under the static provider-major-2 binding and `LocalFromUntrusted` for first-frame faults.\\n- ANOMALY: the sleep-past-deadline test exposed a real defect, not a test artifact — killing only the direct child leaves grandchildren holding the pipes, hanging the wait past the deadline. Fixed with process-group kill on unix (direct-child kill on Windows, stated) plus `WaitDelay` and stream abandonment; the test bounds elapsed time below the drain delay to pin it.\\n- BOUND: provhost cover 86.1% statements; the remainder is constructor-error propagation branches (constructors proven total by an explicit test) and `ExecRunner` stdin-failure races. Full body vocabularies and mutation idempotency receipts belong to the conformance leaf; no §7.2 clause newly discharged (`tracecheck` unchanged at 17/403, 74 cases).\\n- EVIDENCE: 25 narrowing mutants, each verified present, compiling, and restored — 25 killed, 0 survivors. `go test ./...` exit 0 (15/15), `-race` exit 0 in batches over all 15, `go vet` and `GOOS=windows go vet` exit 0, `gofmt` clean, `tracecheck` exit 0, catalog generate current.\\n- STATUS: Ready for review, handed off uncommitted.\\n\\n### 0327 — The fourth enumeration failure: the refusal inventory could derive nothing and pass\\n\\n- SCOPE: `internal/provider` round-3 rework of TASK-260830-2890sd (findings F11–F17).\\n- FINDING: `auditRefusalInventory` derived its domain from package source, then asserted only that every derived site was exercised. Four mutants stayed green: empty domain, 1-of-N domain, skipping `provider.go` entirely, and a planted stray `Error{}` plus an empty-but-successful wrong-cwd read. The same stray alone reddened, which locates the hole precisely in the derivation, not the check. This is entry 0230's defect one level up, and the fourth recorded instance of \\\"closed the guards the review named, did not enumerate the rest\\\" — the package already carried the floor three times (`TestDiscoveryReachesNoProcess`, `TestCandidatesAdvertiseNoCapability`, the source inventory's exact counts) while the identical file-scan idiom in the refusal inventory had none.\\n- FIX: bidirectional inventory — `ScannedFiles` floor (\\\"scanned no production sources; the check is blind\\\"), empty-sites floor (\\\"derived no refusal sites; the scan is blind\\\"), and a reverse check requiring every exercised site to resolve to a derived site. The reverse bound is derived from the test run, not hand-listed, so truncation reddens without a magic number. Floors that must survive early-return mutants live at the caller (`TestSection71AdvertisesNoPublicSDK` for `exportedSymbols`); a floor inside the helper is bypassed by `return nil` at its top. `auditRefusalInventory` floors live in the audit itself, so they hold for the same reason.\\n- FINDING: the production `OSSystem` seam had zero negative tests — eight survivors in one file. Three are entry 0230's uid-0 twin on the attestation side (`Inspect` swallowing the error as uid 0, `fileOwnerUID` returning `0, nil` against its own doc comment, `CurrentOperatorPolicy` seeding `{0}`), one inverts the headline invariant at the only violable point (`ReadDir` error as absence). The `Inspect` attestation-error branch is unreachable with real stat results on unix, so it is forced through a documented `ownerAttester` variable — the only seam that makes the branch testable rather than merely readable.\\n- BOUND: `Geteuid`-vs-`Getuid` is environment-equivalent on any host where euid == uid (here `euid=502 uid=502`, probed); the test pins `OperatorUID` to the effective uid and kills the `{0}`-wildcard mutant, and the residual is recorded, not waved through. Native Windows refusal is covered by a build-tagged test compiled via `GOOS=windows go vet`, not executed here.\\n- EVIDENCE: 19-mutant harness over every rev4 survivor shape: 18 RED, 1 environment-equivalent green (G40b). `go test ./...` exit 0, provider cover 97.0%, `go vet`/`GOOS=windows go vet`/`GOOS=windows go build` clean.\\n- STATUS: Resolved. TASK-260830-2890sd round-3 rework, handed to review uncommitted per the CR-shape instruction.\\n\\n## 2026-09-04\\n\\n### 0230 — The guard the round did not sweep was the one that matters: uid 0 admitted, suite green\\n\\n- SCOPE: `internal/provider` round-2 review of TASK-260830-2890sd at `d8fc669`; §7.1. 32 mutants, 30 caught, 2 survived.\\n- FINDING: injecting `if uid == 0 { return true }` into `OwnerPolicy.Approves` (`provider.go:143`) leaves `go test ./... -count=1` GREEN across all 14 packages. No test in the package uses uid 0 — `fakeUID = 1000`, `foreignUID = 2000`, `adminUID = 7`. The property is asserted three times and measured zero times: the production doc comment at `provider.go:139` (\\\"no superuser exception is implied\\\"), the outcome artifact (\\\"no implied superuser\\\"), and the §7.1 MUST pinned verbatim at `enumeration_test.go:135` (\\\"owned by the operator or an administrator-approved identity\\\"). Root-owned system PATH directories are the first case a real host meets, so this is the realistic identity, not an exotic one. Confirmed by isolation: a probe driving `Discover` with a uid-0 file under an operator-only policy PASSes against intact production and FAILs under the injection.\\n- ROOT CAUSE: entry 1632's generalisation, for the third recorded time. Round 2 closed the four guards the review named and did not enumerate the rest of the path. `OwnerPolicy.Approves` is the next guard inside `trustCandidate` after the two that were fixed, and `TestDiscoverRefusesRelativePluginDir` is the single-entry twin of the multi-entry PATH fixture the same round wrote — narrowing the `plugin_dirs` absolute gate to `index == 0` also survives the full suite. The remedy for \\\"proven at one sample\\\" is the traversal, not the named fix.\\n- NOTE: the rev1 verdict supplied a complete logbook entry with an instruction to land it verbatim, and `LOGBOOK.md` is untouched in `292fc6d..d8fc669`. The lesson that would have prevented this round's omission was written into a board resource and never reached the file the next round reads. A verdict-supplied entry is part of the rework scope, not an appendix to it.\\n- FINDING: two round-2 fixes closed the quoted mutant and not its class. The new 3x4 trust-gate table enumerates its sources from a hand-written literal and reports `12/12` as `len(sources) * len(dimensions)` compared against itself, so adding a fourth discovery source to `Discover` with every trust gate bypassed leaves `go test ./...` GREEN across all 14 packages. The package already owns the right pattern one file away — `refusal_inventory_test.go` derives refusal sites from the AST and reddens naming `provider.go:451` when an unexercised site is injected — and the table should derive its source labels from the `collectDirectory` call sites the same way. Separately, `TestVerifyDetectsLateByteDigestChange` moves the LAST digest byte, so `sum[:1]`, `sum[:16]`, `sum[:31]` and `sum[8:24]` all redden while `sum[1:]` SURVIVES: the guard moved from proven-at-byte-0 to proven-at-byte-31, the same 1-of-32 shape mirrored. A 32-subtest sweep over byte index costs microseconds and reports the ratio the domain actually has.\\n- NOTE: the round-2 fixes are real and were each re-confirmed by re-injecting the round-1 mutant at `d8fc669` — the 12-cell source x dimension table, the PATH absolute gate on both the fake and production `OSSystem` seams, the shape-only `Verify` fixture with per-branch `Detail()` pins, the late-byte digest fixture (kills both `sum[:1]` and `sum[:31]`), and the approved-administrator owner-identity subtest. Production behaviour is correct as written; only the evidence is short.\\n- STATUS: Pending. TASK-260830-2890sd routed to `to-dev`; no production behaviour change required.\\n\\n### 0210 — The inventory gate proves a site was reached, not that a test can tell which site fired\\n\\n- SCOPE: `internal/provider` review of TASK-260830-2890sd at `292fc6d`; §7.1. Five mutants survived the full suite.\\n- ROOT CAUSE: every `Verify` branch reports the same `integrity_failure` code, and every subtest asserts on `Code()` alone. `TestVerifyDetectsSubstitution/replaced_with_directory` (`trust_test.go:136`) sets `fakeFile{content: nil, ..., regular: false}` — two facts at once — so deleting the non-regular check at `provider.go:455-457` leaves the whole suite green. Isolated: with the fixture changed to `content: []byte(\\\"bytes\\\")` the subtest PASSes against intact production and FAILs with the check deleted.\\n- FINDING: this is entry 0115's own defect class, reintroduced one subtest away in the same function within the same commit that recorded fixing it. The refusal-inventory gate cannot see it. The gate requires each constructor site to be REACHED, and this fixture does reach `provider.go:456`; it cannot tell that a different site would have produced the same observable. By construction it also cannot see a DELETED site. Within one code class, \\\"site reached\\\" is not \\\"branch distinguished\\\" — the gate needs the test to assert which fact changed, and no test in the package asserts `Detail()`.\\n- FINDING: the trust gate is proven at 1 of 3 discovery sources. Injecting `if source == \\\"path\\\" { info.IsRegular = true; info.UID = owner.OperatorUID }` into `trustCandidate` survives, as does the same for every `source != \\\"plugin_dirs[0]\\\"`. All four refusal tests use a single `/plugins` directory; PATH is exercised only positively and for duplicates. Entry 1632's rule — enumerate every guard on the path and measure each over its own domain — applies per SOURCE here, not only per guard.\\n- FINDING: `Discover` refuses a relative `providers.plugin_dirs` entry (`provider.go:334`) but never checks `system.PathDirs()` (`provider.go:348`), and `OSSystem.Canonicalize` resolves relative entries against the process working directory via `filepath.Abs`. Probed through production `OSSystem`: `PATH=\\\".\\\"` admits an operator-owned `ax-provider-evil` planted in the CWD; `plugin_dirs=[\\\".\\\"]` refuses it. Two sources sharing `collectDirectory` do not share the gate. The owner check does not help — files in a checkout the operator owns are operator-owned.\\n- FINDING: the digest guard is proven at 1 of 32 bytes; narrowing `provider.go:466` to `sum[:1]` survives, because both fixture pairs happen to differ in their first byte. The `identity != record.owner` half of `provider.go:458` has no test at all — `adminUID = 7` is declared at `fake_test.go:118` and never used, and it is exactly the fixture that case needs.\\n- NOTE: §7.1 has no ownership binding at all — the registry binds 7.2/7.3/7.4/7.5/7.A/7.8/7.9 to the placeholder `catalog.ForRelease` and never mentions 7.1. The deferral reason is accurate (`traceability.go:422` pins the projection to `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256`), but the section is now implemented and must be claimed before the Story closes.\\n- STATUS: Pending. TASK-260830-2890sd routed to `to-dev`; 27 of 32 mutants caught, production behaviour correct as written.\\n\\n### 0115 — Provider discovery leaf: the inventory gate caught a test proving nothing, and a trust test caught a double hash\\n\\n- SCOPE: new `internal/provider` (Discover/Trust/Verify over a filesystem seam), `README.md` section, TASK-260830-2890sd §7.1.\\n- FINDING: first full run was green except the new refusal-inventory gate, which named `provider.go:463` — the Verify re-read failure path. The `unreadable target` subtest set file content to nil without scripting a read error, so it exercised the digest-mismatch path while claiming the re-read path. Same class as entry 1447: a test passing while proving nothing about the path it names. Scripted `contentErr` fixed it; gate green.\\n- FINDING: `TestTrustRecordsCandidateFacts` caught a real double hash before commit — production called `sha256.Sum256` then `scalar.SHA256Digest` (which hashes again). Single `SHA256Digest(content)` now; Verify compares one hash against the receipt in constant time.\\n- DECISION: `ax-provider-<id>` names with an invalid suffix (e.g. `.bak`, uppercase) fail discovery with `invalid_config` rather than being skipped — a prefixed name signals intent and silent skipping would turn a typo'd install into a confusing absence. Bare non-prefixed entries are still ignored. Same-file-twice (two dirs, identical bytes) still fails: the section's MUST is unconditional and order confers no precedence.\\n- BOUND: native Windows owner attestation is unimplemented (`os_windows.go` refuses), so external executables are undiscoverable there; builtins unaffected. Package mutates no durable state — the host persists `TrustRecord`; no crash/idempotency surface inside. Ownership JSON untouched (hash-pinned; another scope's review owns it).\\n- EVIDENCE: `go test ./internal/provider/ -count=1` exit 0 (34 top-level PASS), cover 93.4%, `go vet`/`go build` clean, `GOOS=windows go build` clean, full `go test ./...` exit 0, tracecheck exit 0 unchanged at 17/403.\\n- STATUS: Resolved. TASK-260830-2890sd, handed to review.\\n\\n## 2026-09-03\\n\\n### 1632 — The generalisation was written down one round earlier and still not executed: a list is a forced traversal, prose is a memory\\n\\n- SCOPE: new `internal/cliresult/guards_test.go` and `internal/cliresult/guard_inventory_test.go`, one production fix in `internal/cliresult/client.go`, `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-7 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §1.6, §14.2, §15.1, §15.2, §17.2.\\n- ROOT CAUSE: the `ErrUnregisteredExitStatus` gate in `cliresult.Read` was driven at four sampled values — `{1, 42, -1, 255}` — while its twin `axerror.decodeExitStatus`, which has the same shape and the same domain, was swept over 0..255 in the round-6 rework one file away. Restricting the reader gate to exactly its four covered values left `go test ./... -count=1` at exit 0 across all 13 packages, as did sparing 127 and 137 — the two statuses a wrapper most plausibly returns when `ax` never ran. Reproduced SURVIVED at the reviewed head with the delete-only control KILLED in the same run.\\n- FINDING: entry 1447 below states the exact generalisation this round violated — \\\"when a class of 'proven at one sample' is found, the remedy is to enumerate every guard on that path and measure each over its own domain, not to fix the one that was named\\\". It was written and then not executed. The orchestrator asked for the enumerated list, accepted a sweep without it on the reasoning that the table was a means rather than an end, and the omission it would have made visible cost a whole review round. A generalisation recorded as prose is a memory; a list is a traversal. Working from memory closes the guard you were told about.\\n- FIX: `TestTheRefusalGuardInventoryIsDerivedFromTheReaderSource` parses every refusal site out of `internal/cliresult/client.go` with `go/ast` and requires a bijection with a published README table — twelve rows, each naming its function, sentinel, refusal marker, domain-evidence class from a closed vocabulary, and the test declarations that measure it. A guard added to the reader with no row is a red; a row whose marker no longer resolves is a red. A second table enumerates the guards this leaf added or reordered outside that file, `decodeExitStatus` required in it by name, because the twin one file away is exactly what a single-file table would have missed again.\\n- FINDING: the traversal immediately produced three more instances of the class, none of which any reviewer had named. The `ErrForeignDocument` branch was covered by one schema identifier, so restricting it to that identifier and routing every other foreign schema into `readSuccess` survived the suite. The \\\"schema member is not a string\\\" guard was covered by the number `5`, so restricting it to JSON numbers survived. Both are the round-6 shape: not an admission bypass, a change in WHICH FACT the machine client is told. The presence guard for a missing `schema` member survives an arbitrary predicate keyed on the exit status, which is the class round 4 declined to block on and is recorded as a stated bound rather than fixed.\\n- FIX (production, the only behaviour change this round): `encoding/json` admits JSON `null` into a `string` and yields `\\\"\\\"`, so a document whose `schema` member was `null` had been answered as a FOREIGN CONTRACT carrying the schema `\\\"\\\"` — a claim that some other contract owns the document, when what is true is that this one is not readable. Found only because the type guard's domain was swept rather than sampled. The guard now checks the raw token alongside the unmarshal.\\n- DECISION: the round-6 reviewer asked whether `exitStatusIsNotEnough` should distinguish \\\"this status carries no registered code\\\" from \\\"this status is not in the table\\\", since it computes `len(fanIn[status])` with no membership check and would report a map miss as a measured 0. The answer is not a defensive branch: a branch no production path can take promises nothing and is never measured. `TestTheFanInSentenceCannotReportAMapMissAsAMeasurement` proves the fabrication unreachable instead — `documentSchema` has exactly one caller and it is `Read`, the gate precedes that call in `Read`'s body by AST position, and every (registered version, registered failure status) pair measures at least one code, so a 0 there could only ever have been a miss. Moving the gate behind the discriminator reddens it.\\n- FINDING (reviewer observation, disclosed rather than fixed): the §1.6 common-data-model gate does NOT enforce the number half of that section. `Read` admits a Structured Error whose `details` carry `1.5`, which SPEC.md forbids as integers inside the IEEE 754 safe-integer range. The gate's doc comment enumerates what it owns honestly; its opening sentence and README's matching phrasing overstated it. §1.6's number rule is bound to `internal/scalar` and left unevidenced there, so nothing false is claimed in the traceability projection. The admission is now ASSERTED by a test, so closing the gap reddens the pin and forces the disclosure to be updated in the same change — a disclosure with no pin decays into a paragraph nobody re-reads.\\n- FINDING (reviewer observation, correction to the record): three mutants were dismissed in round 5 on the grounds that \\\"none is a registered code\\\". `terminal_backend_capability_unproven` IS registered at Structured Error 1.3.0 exit 6 (`catalog_gen.go:541`), and sparing it in `ExitCodeFor`'s version scoping survives on a clean tree. The conclusion holds for a different reason — the same narrowing on `observation_gap` is killed by `TestExitCodeForRefusesVersionAndCodeDrift`, so the class has a covered representative. The dismissal stands; the sentence behind it did not. A survivor dismissed with a wrong reason is worse than one dismissed with none, because the reason is what the next round trusts.\\n- DECISION: reviewer observation O3 was closed by measurement rather than by the stated bound the reviewer offered. `verifyEnvelopeIdentity`'s major comparison was proven at major 2 alone; it is now swept over majors 0..64 through `Read` with the bound stated for the tail. Where a domain can be swept cheaply, a stated bound is a bound about effort, and this leaf has published the rule that a bound must be about the contract.\\n- BOUND, unchanged and restated: `tracecheck` still reports `acceptance_cases=74` and `clauses_discharged=17/403`. All eight new tests register against the existing `cli-result-machine-reading` acceptance case rather than new ones, so no coverage figure moved; the registration was probed by renaming a declaration and confirming `tracecheck` names the missing declaration ahead of the projection digest.\\n- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 63 to 86 mutants — 85 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, 64 narrowing by the harness's own definition, suite restored green after every batch. Supersedes the \\\"63 mutants at 62 killed\\\" figure in the 1447 entry below. One mutant on the §1.6 disclosure was DISCARDED as equivalent and replaced: disabling the assertion in the test changes no outcome while the gap is open, so the replacement closes the gap in production and requires the pin to redden. Same stated bound: the harness mutates the tree, lives under `.temp/`, and no committed artifact derives its counts.\\n\\n### 1447 — The exit status a document declares is a member, not a fact: a guard proven on one code let a relabelled envelope forge a retry claim\\n\\n- SCOPE: new `internal/axerror/agreement_test.go` and `internal/cliresult/relabel_test.go`, `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-6 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §15.1, §15.2, §15.3. No production code changed — every round-6 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.\\n- ROOT CAUSE: the code-to-exit-status agreement in `axerror.decodeBody`, `if exitCode != expectedExit`, was covered by exactly one row of `TestDecodeRefusesClosedShapeViolations`: `observation_gap` driven at exit 5 instead of its own exit 9. One code, one wrong status, out of 752 ordered (code, wrong-status) pairs at 1.0.0 and 1,744 at 1.3.0. Narrowing the guard to `code == \\\"observation_gap\\\"` — restricting it to exactly the pair that row drives — left `go test ./... -count=1` at exit 0 across all 13 packages; so did sparing `policy_refused` or `authentication_failed` individually. Only deleting the guard reddened anything. Reproduced SURVIVED at the reviewed head before a line of test code was written.\\n===TRACECHECK===\\n./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck\\n./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-34elja/TASK-260830-34elja_tracecheck-01.log\\n./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2iint0/TASK-260830-2iint0_tracecheck-config.log\\n./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2iint0/TASK-260830-2iint0_tracecheck-section6-refusal-round3.log\\n./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2iint0/TASK-260830-2iint0_tracecheck-config-round3.log\\n./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2iint0/TASK-260830-2iint0_tracecheck-all-round3.log\\n./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2iint0/TASK-260830-2iint0_tracecheck-all-rework.log\\n./.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-1bipsa/TASK-260830-1bipsa_tracecheck-01.log\\n./.task-board/.resources/BUG-260902-2m7slg/BUG-260902-2m7slg_rework-r3-tracecheck.log\\n./.task-board/.resources/BUG-260902-lyhvkw/BUG-260902-lyhvkw_tracecheck-refresh-a351afd.log\\n./LOGBOOK.md:21:- EVIDENCE: 19-mutant verify sweep (`/tmp/rev2-verify.sh`, cp-aside/cp-back per mutant, presence asserted, restore byte-verified): 18 killed, 1 declared equivalent (M09b). True two-site R17 verified killed separately with the reviewer's exact flip. `go test ./...` exit 0 (15/15), `-race` on provhost exit 0, `go vet` and `GOOS=windows go vet` exit 0, gofmt gate clean, `go build` on linux/windows exit 0, `tracecheck` exit 0 unchanged (17/403, 74 cases). provhost cover 86.1% → 88.1%.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:33:- BOUND: provhost cover 86.1% statements; the remainder is constructor-error propagation branches (constructors proven total by an explicit test) and `ExecRunner` stdin-failure races. Full body vocabularies and mutation idempotency receipts belong to the conformance leaf; no §7.2 clause newly discharged (`tracecheck` unchanged at 17/403, 74 cases).\\n./LOGBOOK.md:34:- EVIDENCE: 25 narrowing mutants, each verified present, compiling, and restored — 25 killed, 0 survivors. `go test ./...` exit 0 (15/15), `-race` exit 0 in batches over all 15, `go vet` and `GOOS=windows go vet` exit 0, `gofmt` clean, `tracecheck` exit 0, catalog generate current.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:77:- EVIDENCE: `go test ./internal/provider/ -count=1` exit 0 (34 top-level PASS), cover 93.4%, `go vet`/`go build` clean, `GOOS=windows go build` clean, full `go test ./...` exit 0, tracecheck exit 0 unchanged at 17/403.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:94:- BOUND, unchanged and restated: `tracecheck` still reports `acceptance_cases=74` and `clauses_discharged=17/403`. All eight new tests register against the existing `cli-result-machine-reading` acceptance case rather than new ones, so no coverage figure moved; the registration was probed by renaming a declaration and confirming `tracecheck` names the missing declaration ahead of the projection digest.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:108:- BOUND, unchanged and restated: `tracecheck` still reports `acceptance_cases=74` and `clauses_discharged=17/403`. All three new tests register against existing acceptance cases rather than new ones, so no coverage figure moved; each registration was probed by renaming its declaration and confirming `tracecheck` names the missing declaration ahead of the projection digest.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:122:- BOUND, unchanged and restated: `tracecheck` still reports `acceptance_cases=74` and `clauses_discharged=17/403`. Both new tests register against existing acceptance cases rather than new ones, so no coverage figure moved; the two registrations were probed by renaming each declaration and confirming `tracecheck` names the missing declaration ahead of the projection digest.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:129:- ROOT CAUSE: `README.md` published \\\"73 executable acceptance cases\\\" while `ownership.v0.5.0.json` held 74 entries with 74 distinct ids and `tracecheck` printed `acceptance_cases=74`. The first pass of this leaf moved the registry from 65 to 73 and updated the sentence; the round-2 rework added a case and did not. Nothing measured the figure — setting it to 999 left `go test ./...` and `tracecheck` both at exit 0.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:152:- EVIDENCE: the new test is registered in the `cli-result-machine-reading` acceptance case and the ownership projection digest re-pinned. That registration is not decorative and was not assumed to be checked: renaming the declaration to one that does not exist makes `tracecheck` fail with `declaration ... is absent from \\\"internal/cliresult/client_test.go\\\"` — the declaration-resolution check answers before the digest check does.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:153:- EVIDENCE: `go build`, `go vet`, `gofmt -l ./internal` clean; `go test ./... -count=1` exit 0 across 13 packages; `-cover` exit 0 with `cliresult` 95.5% and `axerror` 99.5% (both unchanged — the sweeps add rows through statements already executed); `tracecheck` exit 0 at `acceptance_cases` 74 and `clauses_discharged` 17/403, both unchanged, because a test added to an existing acceptance case discharges no new clause.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:172:- EVIDENCE: 23 mutants, each verified applied, compiled, and carrying the MUTATED text on disk before measurement; 22 killed, 1 declared subsumed, 0 unexplained survivors. Nine narrow rather than delete. `go build`, `go vet`, `gofmt -l` clean; `go test ./... -count=1` exit 0 across 13 packages; `-cover` exit 0 with `cliresult` 95.5% and `axerror` 99.5%; `tracecheck` exit 0 at `acceptance_cases` 73 → 74 and `clauses_discharged` 17/403 unchanged — the new acceptance case is attached to the §15.1 and §17.2 bindings as evidence and claims no additional clause, because the gate discharges no obligation the clause scanner measures.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:216:- ROOT CAUSE: **a clause was claimed against a topically related section instead of against the actor its MUST binds.** Ownership clause `15.1#6` at SPEC.md:11047 was discharged by `LocalFromUntrusted`. That line's only RFC 2119 sentence is \\\"The plugin MUST NOT masquerade a different-major object as v2\\\" - it binds the **provider plugin**, a child process this repository does not build; its only binaries are `cataloggen` and `tracecheck`. The host half of the same table row, which `LocalFromUntrusted` does implement, carries no RFC 2119 keyword at all, so it is not what the clause scanner measured.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:221:- EVIDENCE: 26 mutants, each verified applied before measurement, 26 killed, 0 survivors, final restore re-verified uncached. `go test ./... -count=1` exit 0 across 12 packages; `go build`, `go vet`, `gofmt -l` clean; `tracecheck` exit 0 at 9/394.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:280:- ROOT CAUSE: `internal/traceability` proved two things about a `section_binding` — the named Go declaration parses and exists, and the linked acceptance case is registered with an executable test. Neither compares the implementation against the section it claims, so a sliver satisfied a whole-section binding and `tracecheck -section X` reported it as owned. `section:2.2` (22 unconditional lease/replica invariants) was bound to `validateSessionRecordCommon`, which validates one enum and a name grammar; `section:18.4` (audit retention) was bound to `OpenProjection`, and no retention code exists. Three independently produced branches reached that state without anyone noticing.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:289:- SCOPE: `internal/traceability/{traceability.go,ownership.v0.5.0.json,cmd/tracecheck}`, `internal/specdoc/specdoc.go` (its \\\"only test binaries import it\\\" claim became false), `README.md`. Three former positive-admission tests inverted into disclosure tables that pin each refusal by exact ratio.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:295:- EVIDENCE: `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck -section 15.2` exited **0**. §15.2 is the nineteen-row normative exit-code registry (SPEC.md:11073-11095) and nothing in the tree implements it — the only `os.Exit` calls are the `exit(1)` paths of `cataloggen` and `tracecheck`. Same for `-section 7.3`, the closed Provider Manifest, whose sole trace is a catalog row naming its URN. Both were bound to `internal/catalog/catalog.go:ForRelease`, an unrelated symbol.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:301:- GUARD ADDED: `internal/specdoc` reaching non-test code made \\\"the embedded document never reaches the ax command\\\" a claim with nothing behind it. `TestEmbeddedDocumentNeverReachesAProductBinary` now reads the module import graph from source with `go/parser`, refuses any `main` outside `tracecheck` that can reach the package, and proves the detector by planting a `cmd/ax` that imports `internal/traceability`. `TestModuleHasNoProductCommandYet` fails when `ax` lands, forcing that decision to be deliberate.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:324:- STATUS: `internal/canonicaljson` 97.2%, `internal/specdoc` 100.0%. gofmt, `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `go test ./... -count=1`, `go test ./... -cover`, `tracecheck`, `go generate ./internal/catalog` all exit 0. Ready for review.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:356:- STATUS: BUG-260902-1874eo and sibling BUG-260902-9uwnm7 handed to review in one change. `gofmt`, `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `go test ./... -count=1`, `go test ./... -cover -count=1`, tracecheck, `go generate ./internal/catalog` + clean diff, and `FuzzCanonicalizeRoundTrip` at 100x all exit 0. `internal/canonicaljson` coverage 97.2%.\\n./LOGBOOK.md:371:- STATUS: `internal/canonicaljson` 97.2% unchanged, `internal/specdoc` 100.0%. gofmt, `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `go test ./... -count=1`, `-cover -count=1`, `tracecheck`, `go generate ./internal/catalog` + `git diff --exit-code` all exit 0. Ready for review.\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 37198,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 9300,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}"}}}
